        "hasCode": true,
        "reasoning": "I'm planning out a static six-wheeled railgun carrier model, roughly 12m long, 3m wide, and 3.5m tall, with a cab at the front and three wheel axles, keeping the design joint-free since it's a static prop.\n\nI'm sketching the railgun on top mounted on a cradle extending beyond the cab, plus four deployable stabiliser legs with ground pads, and setting up box/cylinder helper functions with a flat olive-toned color palette for construction.\n\nNow I'm building the chassis frame and lower hull as boxes, then roughing out the upper hull body with slanted panel boxes to approximate sloped armor plating.\n\nWorking out wheel placement next -- six wheels across three axles per side, each wheel made from a tire cylinder, a steel hub, lug caps, and a ring of small tread blocks -- then sketching the cab box forward of the hull.\n\nNow I'm planning the railgun assembly: twin rails mounted on pylons above the cab roof, a rear breech block, and capacitor bank cylinders along the hull sides feeding into the rail coils.\n\nFor stability I'm adding four hydraulic outrigger legs with pivot boxes, angled rod struts, and circular landing pads, plus front and rear bumpers with headlight and hazard-stripe details.\n\nI'm naming the hull side panels distinctly for texture mapping (hullSideL/hullSideR), making sure wheel cylinders sit flush with the ground plane, and finalizing the overall rail height before writing out the static root structure compactly.\n\nNow I'm working out the wheel assembly per axle and side — tire, hub, cap, bolt ring arranged radially around the hub face, and tread blocks spaced around the tire circumference.\n\nAdjusting the radius to .68 keeps the tread sticking out just slightly without clipping through the floor, which is acceptable given the model's overall scale. With that settled, I'll move on to writing out the 8 add operations and 3 textures.\n\n"
      },
